sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Is there a universal metabolic scaling exponent?" ;
    dct:description "Does basal metabolic rate scale with body mass with a single exponent across taxa, and if so is it 3/4 (fractal supply networks) or 2/3 (surface-to-volume), or does the exponent vary systematically with taxon, temperature and body size?" ;

    skos:historyNote "Known state: Kleiber (1932) reported 3/4; West, Brown and Enquist (1997) derived 3/4 from space-filling branching networks; White and Seymour (2003) found 2/3 for mammals once body temperature and digestive state are controlled; Kozlowski and Konarzewski (2004) disputed the WBE derivation. Current syntheses find curvature and taxon dependence. Thompson's surface-volume claims (ch02-01, ch03-07) encode the 2/3 alternative." ;
    dct:subject "biology" ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "open-problem/tier-B" ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resolved when: A pre-registered phylogenetically controlled analysis across the organism taxa with AnAge/PanTHERIA body mass and metabolic data, with stated predictions: a single exponent with 95% interval excluding one of 2/3 and 3/4, or a model-comparison verdict that a curved or taxon-specific model beats any single power law." ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resolved when: A Lean 4 formalization of the WBE derivation stating its assumptions as hypotheses, so the disputed step is explicit." ;
    skos:scopeNote "Refuted if: If the pooled exponent interval contains both 2/3 and 3/4 after controls, the universality question is empirically underdetermined with present data." ;
    skos:note "Data access: AnAge (open), PanTHERIA (open), White and Seymour 2003 compilation; taxon resolution via test-data/organisms." ;
    skos:note "Requires: phylogenetic-comparative-methods" ;
    skos:note "Requires: allometry" ;
    skos:note "Requires: data-test" ;
    skos:note "Requires: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Scale: organism" ;
    skos:note "Scale: evolutionary" ;
    skos:note "Also recorded as a Delphi Frontier Problem: Mammalian body size control" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch02-01> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-10> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.3733/hilg.v06n11p315> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0436428100> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.0269-8463.2004.00830.x> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/problems.json> .

  <https://doi.org/10.3733/hilg.v06n11p315> dct:title "Body size and metabolism" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Kleiber 1932, Body size and metabolism" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> dct:title "A General Model for the Origin of Allometric Scaling Laws in Biology" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "West, Brown and Enquist 1997, A general model for the origin of allometric scaling laws in biology" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0436428100> dct:title "Mammalian basal metabolic rate is proportional to body mass\n                    <sup>2/3</sup>" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "White and Seymour 2003, Mammalian basal metabolic rate is proportional to body mass 2/3" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.0269-8463.2004.00830.x> dct:title "Is West, Brown and Enquist's model of allometric scaling mathematically correct and biologically relevant?" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Kozlowski and Konarzewski 2004, Is West, Brown and Enquist's model of allometric scaling mathematically correct and biologically relevant?" .
}

